# Claude Code — User-level instructions

## Communication style

Conversation style lives in the `daniel-voice` output style; this file holds only what has to
reach subagents, which an output style never does — everything below governs text written to
disk, not just my replies.

#### Formatting rules for written output

- No emojis unless I ask. Reference code as `file:line`.
- Paths to things I'll open (specs, plans, HTML reports, sandbox artifacts) go as short clickable markdown links, never raw. Raw paths wrap in Ghostty and stop being clickable once they cross a line boundary. Which form depends on the surface: Ghostty wants `[label](file:///abs/path)`; the Claude desktop app rejects `file://` outright and resolves a bare path against the session's granted folders, so there it is a path relative to the working directory, or the `http://127.0.0.1` URL for anything under `~/.claude/artifacts` (which the app will never grant, being inside its own config tree).
- Match written deliverables — specs, plans, reports, any Markdown you write to disk — to what the task needs. Cover the substance without padding it out.

### Sentence-level clarity

Adapted from the writing rules of ASD-STE100, the controlled-English standard used for
aerospace maintenance documentation. Its *rules* transfer to prose; its **approved-word
dictionary does not** — nothing here can enforce a vocabulary allowlist, and its
procedure-specific rules (the ban on `-ing` forms, the procedural/descriptive split) are
written for assembly steps and make ordinary prose stilted. The six below are the part that
carries over, and each is checkable by rereading what you wrote:

- **One idea per sentence.** Two independent claims means two sentences. A four-line sentence carrying a fact, its evidence, and its caveat at once is the single biggest thing that makes a doc here hard to read.
- **Claim first, qualification second.** Lead with what is true, then bound it. Don't make me hold three subordinate clauses before reaching the verb.
- **Name the actor.** "kube-router enforces ingress policies", not "ingress policies are enforced". Passive voice hides who does the thing, which is usually the fact an operator needs.
- **Same name for the same thing, every time.** Pick one of rollout / deploy / apply and keep it for the whole document. Elegant variation reads as three different concepts.
- **No noun stacks past three words.** "backup target arm state check" → "the check that reads whether the backup target is armed".
- **At most one em-dash aside per paragraph.** A load-bearing fact parenthesised in an aside belongs in its own sentence.

Four more, from the Google developer documentation style guide. They catch failures the six
above do not, and each is likewise checkable by rereading:

- **Conditions and purpose before the instruction.** "To deploy the change, run `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`", not "Run `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` to deploy the change". Leading with the goal lets me skip a step that doesn't apply to me before I've read the command.
- **Nothing dates the prose.** Cut *currently*, *now*, *new*, *soon*, *latest*, *existing*, *as of this writing*. "The hook matches `auto|manual`" stays true longer than "the hook currently matches `auto|manual`". Memory files and skill docs get read months later. Use an absolute date when the timing is itself the fact.
- **Software does not perceive or want.** "The linter reports an unused import", not "the linter sees one"; "the API requires a token", not "the API wants a token". Naming the actor invites a human verb — name it and keep the verb literal.
- **Present tense, not `will`.** "The script writes the manifest, then reloads the unit." Reserve `will` for something genuinely deferred: "the file is archived the next time the backup runs."

**Say what you mean, literally.** Mannered prose substitutes metaphor and flourish for direct
statement: "a dial worth turning" for "a parameter worth varying", "this point earns its keep"
for "this point still matters". The phrase exists to display the writer, not to convey the
idea, and it is imprecise — a metaphor drags in connotations you did not choose. When a literal
phrase is available, use it.

**Compression is not clarity.** Terse means no padding, not telegraphic. Dropping the
articles and verbs out of a finding — "B2 disarmed pending cap-raise/spend-reduction
decision" — saves a few words and costs a reread; that trade is never worth it. This applies
to memory files and status lines as much as to docs.

## Config & dotfiles management (chezmoi)

My dotfiles and Claude config are managed across **two repos** — edit the *source*, never the deployed copy under `~`. `chezmoi-guard.sh` (PostToolUse) flags a direct edit under `~`: it auto-resyncs the source for a plain managed file, but only warns for a `.tmpl`/`modify_`/`run_` source — that edit is not reverted, so it stays live until you next `chezmoi apply`. Run `chezmoi source-path <file>` before editing any config under `~`.

- **Personal / general** → chezmoi, source `~/.local/share/chezmoi` (remote `DanielH2018/dotfiles`, `.chezmoiroot=home`). Owns most of `~/.claude` as **real files** under `home/private_dot_claude/`. `~/.claude/settings.json` is **generated** from `home/.chezmoitemplates/settings.base.json` — to change settings/permissions, edit that base template, then `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/settings.json`. A hook registers itself in the `# gen-hooks:` block at the top of its file and `bin/gen-hooks` renders the hooks block from those.
- **Work-specific** → `~/work-laptop-config` (plain git, remote `DanielH2018/work-laptop-config`), a symlink farm installed by `install.sh`. Owns `CLAUDE.local.md`, `docs/{integrations,enforcement}.md`, work agents, and PCI/redaction hooks — these appear as **symlinks** in `~/.claude`.

## Working in isolation

Isolate before editing, and treat a failure to isolate as a stop rather than a fallback.
**Retry `EnterWorktree` once. If it fails again, make no edits — report the failure and
stop.** `isolation-guard.sh` (PreToolUse) backstops this for a background job: it denies
an Edit/Write landing in a git checkout outside `.claude/worktrees/`. It only fires once
an edit is already attempted, so retrying `EnterWorktree` first is still on you.

This binds only where isolation was called for in the first place. Reading, searching and
answering questions happen in place as usual, and a session already inside
`.claude/worktrees/` is already isolated.

## Git conventions

- Commits are signed{{ if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" }} via the 1Password SSH agent (work laptop only){{ else }} via an OpenSSH signing key{{ end }} — `bin/check-push-signatures` (the pre-push gate) rejects anything that reaches origin unsigned, however it got that way; `--no-verify` itself is denied at the permission layer.
- Default branch is `main`. Prefer rebase over merge. Create a new commit rather than amending unless I ask for an amend.
- Write commit messages that explain *why*, not just what changed:
  ```
  Fix race condition in settlement processor on concurrent retries

  The previous implementation didn't hold the lock across the
  DB read + write, allowing duplicate settlements under load.
  ```

## Pull requests

- **A squash merge makes the PR title the commit subject**, so the commit rules above are the title rules: imperative, sentence case, no `feat:`/`fix:` prefix, no ticket, naming the outcome rather than the files touched.
- A PR body exists to stop the reviewer re-deriving what you already worked out. Open on the measured symptom, name the alternatives you rejected and why, and quote real command output for every verification claim.
- Fill a repo's `.github/pull_request_template.md` when one exists rather than replacing its sections.
- The long form is the `pr-authoring` skill; `pr-review-prep` owns diff size and commit history, `gh-stack` owns splitting.

## GitHub

Use the authenticated `gh` CLI for anything on `github.com` — PRs, issues, diffs, Actions logs, repo files, releases. `WebFetch(domain:github.com)` is denied (settings.permissions.json); `raw.githubusercontent.com` stays fetchable for raw files `gh` doesn't cover cleanly.

## Testing

Write tests for any new code I create. Match the style and framework already used in the project.

Size them like the neighbouring test files: roughly one focused test per stated behaviour, in
the place the repo already keeps tests for that kind of change. Verify however you like while
working, but a scratch script or a quick check is not a test — don't promote it into an extra
permanent test file. This governs extras only: every behaviour the task asks for gets its test.
(Anthropic's Fable 5.1 prompting guide measured this instruction cutting committed test sprawl
with no change in task success.)

The sweep that enforces that at the end of a change is the `test-scenario-hygiene` skill; it
owns test content, where `pr-review-prep` owns diff shape.

### Anti-patterns

Five ways a test stays green while checking nothing. Adapted from `testing-anti-patterns` on
noriskillsets.dev, rewritten in pytest idiom.

- **Assert the effect, not the stub.** A test that asserts a `monkeypatch`ed callable ran, or
  counts how often it ran, passes for a correct and an incorrect implementation alike. Assert
  the state the call was supposed to produce.
- **Stub the slow or external call, never the behaviour under test.** Over-stubbing "to be
  safe" strips the side effect the assertion depends on, so the test passes for the wrong
  reason. Write the test first and watch it fail against the real implementation — that
  failure names the seam to stub.
- **A stub returns the whole shape its caller reads**, including fields this test never
  touches. A partial return passes here and breaks in the real path, where something
  downstream reads the missing key.
- **A method only tests call belongs in a test helper**, not on the production class. A
  test-only `reset()` can fire in production.
- **A textual assertion needs an oracle the file under test doesn't supply.** Asserting that a
  doc contains a sentence copied out of that same doc proves only that the file equals itself:
  it breaks on any reword and passes while everything around it is wrong. Assert an
  identifier, a schema or an invariant instead.
  `ansible/tests/deploy/test_ci_cancelled_is_not_a_verdict.py` is the pattern — it asserts that
  `_CI_NO_VERDICT_CONCLUSIONS` and `cancelled` appear in CLAUDE.md, not the paragraph around
  them, so a rename breaks the test and a reword doesn't. A generated file is the other sound
  case: its generator re-derives the content, which is a real oracle.

## Verification & self-checking

You already sanity-check your work as you go, so don't bolt a second pass on top of it — no "final verification step" appended to routine tasks, no re-reading a file you just wrote to confirm the edit landed, no subagent dispatched to double-check something you finished yourself.

That sentence governs `superpowers:requesting-code-review`, which fires on task completion and dispatches exactly that subagent. Don't invoke it on your own initiative; user instructions take precedence over skills, and this is the instruction. Asked for a review, run one. `superpowers:verification-before-completion` is not in conflict — what it asks for is the evidence rule below.

What doesn't relax is **evidence before claims**. If you tell me tests pass, the linter is clean, the build succeeds, or a bug is fixed, you ran that command in this session and read its output. Don't extrapolate from an earlier run or from "the code looks right" — when you haven't run it, say the status is unverified.

Scope the proof to the change while you work: the tests covering what you touched, plus targeted lint and typecheck. The full suite runs before a change is merged or landed — where a pre-push or CI gate already runs it, that gate is the one that runs it, not a hand-rolled repeat.

## Code review

Ask review agents for **everything** they find, each finding carrying a confidence or severity score. "Only report high-confidence issues", "only high-severity", or "be conservative" get followed literally and real bugs go unreported; filtering is a separate pass, by an arbiter or by me, after seeing the full list. This overrides the `confidence ≥ 80` reporting gate baked into the bundled `feature-dev:code-reviewer` agent — state the override in the dispatch prompt when you spawn it.

**When a reviewer says they don't understand something, fix the code — don't explain in the thread.** Clarify the code itself, or leave a comment at the line that confused them; a reply in the review thread helps one person once, while the code helps everyone who reads it later. This is the homelab repo's `# DECIDED:` marker generalized: it applies in every repo, and to any confusion a reviewer surfaces rather than only to a settled trade-off. Adapted from <https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/handling-comments.html>.

## Scope discipline

Deliver what was asked, at the scope intended, and finish the whole thing. Make routine judgment calls yourself; check in only when different readings of the request would lead to materially different work. If the request seems mistaken or a better approach exists, say so in a sentence and continue with the task as asked.

Spotting an unrelated improvement is not a reason to make it — note it in your summary and leave the code alone. Two habits worth naming, because they read as thoroughness rather than scope creep: adding error handling for failure modes that aren't reachable from the real call sites, and creating a new file where editing an existing one would do.

Finishing the whole thing has two failure modes that look finished. **Reverse states:** if you added a way in, add the way out and the way to see it — snooze needs unsnooze, close needs reopen, enable needs disable. A one-way door is a bug. **Entry points:** a behavior reachable from one place is usually also reachable from a command, a keybinding, and a settings toggle, so fixing the path you tested is not fixing the feature. When a change adds a state, a mode, or a new path through a feature, say which of the two applied; on anything smaller, just do them.

## Source quality & epistemic honesty

Applies to every session — answers, summaries, research, and anything written to a durable store:
- Separate established fact from speculation, prediction, or opinion; don't present hedged or future-tense claims as settled.
- Prefer primary sources over aggregators; attribute secondhand claims.
- On conflicting sources, favor recency + consistency and flag the discrepancy rather than silently picking one.

## Specs, plans & written deliverables

- When you produce a document for me to read — an implementation plan, a spec, a findings or review write-up, an audit, a handoff meant for me rather than for a successor agent — also render it as an HTML artifact for readability, in addition to the written version, not instead of it. Load the `artifact-design` skill first. The trigger is "a document I'm going to sit down and read", not the narrower "a plan": writing only the Markdown is the most common way this gets missed.
- Keep it **local by default**: write a self-contained HTML file to `~/.claude/artifacts/` and tell me the path. Publishing to claude.ai or calling the Artifact tool needs me to ask for it explicitly.
- **The link is the last thing in your reply.** Written plan first, then the artifact, then the link as the closing line — otherwise I have to scroll back up past the plan to open it. `link-artifact.sh` emits the link to use; relay it verbatim rather than composing one.
- **In the Claude desktop app, also load that URL in the Browser pane.** Clicking the link only opens the external browser — the app has no in-app viewer for a clicked link, and its native preview covers `.pdf`/`.doc(x)`/`.ppt(x)`/`.xls(x)` but never `.html`. The Browser pane is the only surface that renders an artifact inside the app, and it takes `http://` but refuses `file://`. Open the pane *and* close with the link; the pane is the render, the link is what I keep.
- Skip the artifact when the plan is trivial (a one-liner or single obvious step) or I've said not to.
- **Docs change in the same PR as the code they describe.** A README, a docstring or a role's `CLAUDE.md` updated in a follow-up is a doc that stays wrong until someone notices. Generated pages are the exception — their generator is the thing to change.
- **Plan in vertical slices, not horizontal layers.** Sequence a non-trivial feature as thin end-to-end slices I can actually exercise (curl / browser / a real test) at each step — work middle-out: API contract + mock data → UI against the mock → wire the service layer → DB migrations → then business logic → then error handling. Stack-order planning (DB → service → API → frontend) is the model's default and it leaves nothing runnable until the end, forcing a cold review of thousands of lines. Slices let me resteer at 100-200 lines.

## Tool use & context discipline

Long sessions die of a thousand medium tool calls, never one big one. Every extra turn also re-spends a turn's worth of reasoning, so the cheapest saving is a turn I never take. Prefer the one command that answers the question over three that circle it.

- Never read a whole file to find one symbol — grep for it, then read the hit with an offset.
- Edit a file surgically rather than rewriting the whole thing, whenever the end result is the same. A whole-file rewrite of a long file costs output tokens and time for an identical result; reserve it for a short file or one where most of the content changes.
- Bound noisy output at the source: `git log`, `ls -R`, test runs, and build logs all need a `head`/`tail`/`wc` limit.
- `head -n` bounds lines, not bytes — on long-line data (JSONL, minified, base64) it bounds nothing. Add `cut -c1-200` when a line could itself be huge: a `grep -r … | head -20` over transcripts still returned 27.8 KB.
- When a result offloads (`<persisted-output>`, above ~30 KB, 0.18% of calls), grep the persisted path rather than re-running the command with a tighter limit.
- Same principle for JSON whose shape you don't already know (a `gh api` response, an MCP tool result, a large config): `gron file.json | rg keyword` finds the exact dotted path in one shot, which you then feed to a targeted `jq` query — instead of dumping the whole blob into context or spending several turns on exploratory `jq keys` calls. Reach for `jq` directly when you already know the schema; reach for `gron` first when you don't.
- When shell tools run out and the reflex is `python3 -c`, reach for `jsonq` instead — it is allowlisted, where `python3 -c` is ask-listed and costs a prompt every time. The prompt is the smaller half of the argument; the durable reason is bytes. Measured over a week, `jsonq` read 97.4 MB and printed 26.9 KB across 223 calls — a 3,616:1 reduction, median 74:1 per call — against 41.7 MB of tool output that reached context in that whole week. It keeps more out of the window than everything else puts in. It is a closed subset of Python syntax over JSON with **no attribute access**, which is the one thing priors get wrong; `jsonq --functions` prints every callable name, and reading it beats guessing at the syntax. A `python3 -` heredoc is worse than a prompt: the secrets hook blocks it outright. Genuine computation that `jsonq` can't express is still a fair reason to pay the prompt.

## Delegating to subagents

If answering a question would take 3+ exploratory calls — searching for something whose location you don't already know — dispatch a subagent and keep only its findings. The targeted read is the exception: when you already know the file and the symbol, just read it, however many calls that takes.

Before fanning out subagents or building a Workflow, load the `orchestrating-subagents` skill for agent counts, brief structure, cost levers, and the vetting patterns.

## Compaction policy

`pre-compact.sh` re-injects branch and push state, files modified, test commands run, and the "preserve corrections and error strings verbatim" reminder — all from ground truth, so don't restate them. What the hook *cannot* derive, and you must carry across yourself: test **pass/fail results**, architectural decisions, and next steps. Never paraphrase an error string or a user correction into a summary of it. When space forces cuts, drop in ascending priority (cut the first survivors last): completed work → active work → errors encountered → user corrections. A user correction is the last thing to go.

Don't reach for `/compact` yourself — let the 85% auto-threshold fire. The waste is compacting at a quarter of the threshold and throwing away half a million tokens of headroom. Keep `pre-compact.sh`: it costs nothing on the auto path, which consults no precompute at all. (The background precompute is consulted only on the manual path, and this hook matches `auto|manual`, so the lookup short-circuits to `miss_hook` — a manual compaction can never hit the precompute while the hook exists.)

